  "slug": "sound-behaviour-9d6a",
  "summary": "Life stage, food availability, habitat and chemical exposure each alter how aquatic animals respond to stressors, according to four recent studies.",
  "synthesis": "The four items examine how stressors alter performance in aquatic animals, with responses depending on context rather than being fixed. In the Oikopleura dioica preprint, anthropogenic noise changes behaviour in a life stage- and signal-specific way, with larvae appearing most sensitive. A meta-analysis of freshwater invertebrates and fish, also a preprint, reports that sublethal chemical exposure lowers heat tolerance and increases its variability. In the published study of the leather star Dermasterias imbricata, food availability modifies immune, behavioural, metabolic and survival responses to prolonged thermal stress. In published work on cyprinid fish, hypoxia tolerance and oxygen supply capacity differ between lentic and lotic species. The new element is explicit treatment of contextual modifiers, including life stage, nutritional state and habitat, as determinants of stress responses. The Oikopleura and meta-analysis findings remain provisional, and sound-specific evidence is limited to the Oikopleura preprint.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
